In the context of these general questions, the following research hypotheses will be analyzed in this thesis:
%[itemsep=2pt,parsep=2pt]
   \begin{itemize}[label= $ $,topsep=0pt, partopsep=0pt,itemsep=0.5 pt,parsep= 0.5 pt]
   
    \item $H_{1}$:  NPL are  mainly determined by bank-specific and macroeconomic factors
    \item $H_{2}$:  Business cycle indicators are significantly related to NPL
    \item $H_{3}$:  Inflation rates  are significantly related to NPL
    \item $H_{4}$:  Public finance indicators are significantly related to NPL
    \item $H_{5}$:  Terms of trade indicators are significantly related to NPL
    \item $H_{6}$:  The size of a bank is significantly related to NPL
    \item $H_{7}$:  Legal aspects of credit access and  NPL resolution are significantly related to NPL
    \item $H_{8}$:  Bank profitability and cost efficiency indicators are significantly related to NPL
    \item $H_{9}$:  Bank capital adequacy ratios are significantly related to NPL
    \item $H_{10}$: The level of geographic diversification in portfolio is significantly related to NPL
    \item $H_{11}$: Supervision under the SSM  has significant effects on NPL
    \item $H_{12}$: Magnitude and direction of the effects on NPL varies across exposure typesThe effects on NPL varies conditional on the  initial level of NPL
    \item $H_{13}$: The effects on NPL vary conditional on the  initial level of NPL

 \end{itemize} 

Additionally, the findings in this thesis might also provide interesting insights for  supervisory and regulatory authorities in the EU banking system as well as scholars interested in the NPL literature. Firstly, European supervisory authorities and Central Banks might find the results relevant for the definition of Regulatory requirements or general standards with respect to lending policies, capital requirements and overall banking operations. In particular, the results might also be interesting for the preparation of recommendations on NPL strategies, Early Warning Indicators (EWI) or bank-stress testing methodologies drafted by the competent supervisory bodies over banks operations in any of the EU member states. Finally, the findings also provide new contributions in the academic research on NPL in terms of the granularity and novelty of the data (EBA's EU-wide Transparency Exercise), the empirical methodology (quantile regression methods) and the focus on the disaggregated aspects of loan portfolios.

